==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|binoculaire}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/bɪˈnɒkjʊlɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-binocular.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Using two [[eyes]] or viewpoints; especially, using two eyes or viewpoints to ascertain distance.
#: {{co|en|a '''binocular''' microscope or telescope}}
#* {{RQ:Derham Physico-Theology|passage=Most animals are '''binocular'''.}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2013-07-09|author=Joselle DiNunzio Kehoe| title=Cognition, brains and Riemann| site=plus.maths.org| url=http://plus.maths.org/content/cognition-brains-and-riemann| accessdate=2013-09-08
|passage=our perception of distance arises from the geometry of '''binocular''' vision and our early learning seems based on calculating probabilities.}}

